# Line endings, decided here rather than by each machine's git config.
#
# Without this file, `core.autocrlf` decides — and it is true on the machines
# this repo is developed on, so a fresh Windows checkout materialises CRLF while
# the index holds LF. That is not cosmetic. Two gate assertions matched a bare
# `\n` against file contents and therefore passed in a working tree and failed
# on a clean clone of the same commit: the check was reporting on how the
# repository had been checked out, not on the code. See 1e3900f9.
#
# The index is already uniformly LF (587/587 tracked files at the time of
# writing), so this changes no content — `git add --renormalize .` reports zero
# modified files. What it changes is the working directory on the next
# checkout, which is the thing that was wrong.
#
# The `\r?\n` fixes in the gate scripts are deliberately kept. They are
# redundant once this file is honoured, and they cover the two cases it does
# not: a clone made before these attributes existed, and any file type the
# rules below happen to miss.

* text=auto eol=lf

# ── must be CRLF ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# cmd.exe's parser is genuinely line-ending sensitive; a batch file with LF-only
# endings misbehaves around labels and multi-line blocks. `scripts/allow-lan.cmd`
# is the one such file today. `.bat` is listed alongside it because the next one
# will not necessarily be a `.cmd`.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# PowerShell parses LF perfectly well, so this is not a correctness rule — it is
# a "change nothing that already works" rule. All three scripts here
# (allow-lan, start-gateway-provisioner, install-gateway-provisioner-task) are
# Windows-only deployment helpers that have always been CRLF in the working
# directory. There is no benefit to flipping them and a small, dull risk in
# doing so.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# ── must be LF ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# None are tracked today. Listed because a shell script that acquires CRLF fails
# with `bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M`, which is an unpleasant thing to debug and a
# trivial thing to prevent.
*.sh    text eol=lf

# ── generated: normalised, but not worth reading as a diff ───────────────────
# The three package-lock.json files.
#
# Deliberately `-diff` rather than `binary`. They are text, they are already LF,
# and marking them binary would freeze their line endings and make a dependency
# change unreviewable. `-diff` keeps the normalisation and suppresses only the
# thousands of lines of diff nobody reads.
package-lock.json  text eol=lf -diff

# ── binary: never normalised ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# None of these are tracked today, and they are listed for one specific reason:
# `.tools/backups/*.dump` exists on disk and is gitignored. If one is ever
# force-added, normalising a custom-format pg_dump would corrupt it silently —
# the file would still restore-check until the moment it did not.
*.dump  binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.pdf   binary
